        <p>
        Immediately after his return to Apple in 1997 Steve Jobs simplified Apple’s product range and made it more transparent. He cancelled production of the Performa series of computers and looked for a replacement at the same price.

The introduction of the iMac in 1998 had an impact not only on Apple but on the entire computer industry. It was the first &quot;all in one” computer.  Aesthetically it was stunning. The transparent blue plastic looked like an egg, and there was a handle on the top for easy carrying. The front featured headphone jacks, built-in speakers and a CD drive. The iMac wasn’t equipped with the ports that were common for Macintoshes at the time (i.e. SCSI). Instead it featured a new and not widely supported port called a USB.

Likewise, the iMac did not have a floppy drive, which was a much-discussed move - in the early years after it was introduced users could buy an external one that was attached by USB. Apple confidently declared that &quot;the back of our computer looks better than the front of anyone else’s&quot; – a dig at the grey design of Windows PCs. Its bright colour facilitated its advertising. It simply stood out from the rest. Within several months it was the best-selling machine in Japan and the U.S. This was greatly helped by the far-reaching campaign &quot;Think Different&quot;, in which Apple used the familiar faces of prominent personalities.

The &quot;i&quot; in the name stood for ‘internet’ and referred to the ease and speed of internet connection. Since then, Apple has used the letter ‘i’ in many other products and people now use it in everyday speech.
    </p>
